      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-251060",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/a7/a79c385b562066a0567e33928440b2b837e587ebc18aee351899785785218190.png)\n\nSuppose we fix the following order of the vertices in the above graph: $v_{1}$, $v_{2}$, $v_{3}$, $v_{4}$, $v_{5}, v_{6}$.\nThe breadth-first search algorithm for finding the shortest path is applied to the graph, starting at $v_{5}$.\nAfter considering all the vertices that are connected to $v_{5}$, we have the following configuration, where the queue and the array of visited vertices are ordered from left to right:\n\n| Queue: | $v_{3}$, $v_{4}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\n|  | $v_{1}$ | $v_{2}$ | $v_{3}$ | $v_{4}$ | $v_{5}$ | $v_{6}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Visited |  |  | $\u2713$ | $\u2713$ | $\u2713$ |  |\n| Distances | $\u221e$ | $\u221e$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\u221e$ |\n| Previous |  |  | $v_{5}$ | $v_{5}$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\nOn the next step of the algorithm, we pop the vertex\n{{field-1}}\nfrom the head of queue, visit its unvisited neighbors\n{{field-2}},\nand push them into the end of queue. Set the distance to these vertices equal to\n{{field-3}}\nand the previous vertex to be\n{{field-4}}."
    },
