      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-262345",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/2c/2c6bb2b9e936d83c39942f50eda55e2a89973be8d8073adb67043cf630706311.png)\n\nSuppose we fix the following order of the vertices in the above graph: $v_{1}$, $v_{2}$, $v_{3}$, $v_{4}$, $v_{5}$.\nThe breadth-first search algorithm for finding the shortest path is applied to the graph, starting at $v_{4}$.\nAfter two steps of the algorithm, we have the following configuration, where the queue and the array of visited vertices are ordered from left to right:\n\n| Queue: | $v_{5}$, $v_{1}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\n|  | $v_{1}$ | $v_{2}$ | $v_{3}$ | $v_{4}$ | $v_{5}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Visited | $\u2713$ | $\u2713$ |  | $\u2713$ | $\u2713$ |\n| Distances | $2$ | $1$ | $\u221e$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| Previous | $v_{2}$ | $v_{4}$ |  | $-1$ | $v_{4}$ |\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\nOn the next step of the algorithm, we pop the vertex\n{{field-1}}\nfrom the head of queue, visit its unvisited neighbor\n{{field-2}},\nand push it into the end of queue. Set the distance to this vertex equal to\n{{field-3}}\nand the previous vertex to be\n{{field-4}}."
